# Build-context exclusions. One context (the repo root) feeds both the
# API and the frontend stages of Dockerfile, so this file covers both.
#
# Two entries below are load-bearing rather than cosmetic; see the flagged blocks.

# ---- VCS, tooling, editor ---------------------------------------------------
.git
.github
.gitignore
.dockerignore
.claude
.pytest_cache
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]

# ---- Virtualenvs (hundreds of MB, and Linux images cannot use a macOS one) ---
venv/
.venv/
env/

# ---- Node ------------------------------------------------------------------
# node_modules is reinstalled from package-lock.json by `npm ci` in the builder
# stage; a host copy is both huge and possibly the wrong platform.
frontend/node_modules/
frontend/dist/

# frontend/.env: THE ENTRY MOST WORTH KEEPING.
# It is gitignored, so it is in no checkout, but it is on every developer
# machine and holds http://localhost:8000. `vite build` reads .env files, not
# only `vite dev`, so a `COPY frontend/ .` that picked this up would bake a
# stale localhost URL into a shipped image
# regardless of the VITE_API_BASE_URL build ARG. Excluded by name, not merely
# by excluding node_modules. .env.example stays out too; it is documentation
# and Vite would not read it anyway.
frontend/.env
frontend/.env.*

# ---- Generated artefacts the build REGENERATES, and must not inherit --------
# outputs/ and data/cache/ are the two runtime-required, gitignored files
# (see the Dockerfile header). Excluding them is what makes every build behave
# like a clean checkout: a developer machine holding a trained model and a
# precomputed cache cannot leak them into the image and hide the fact that the
# builder stage is what actually produces them.
outputs/
data/cache/

# ---- Not needed by any image ------------------------------------------------
tests/
docs/
README.md
ace-startup-guide.md
render.yaml
docker-compose.yml
Dockerfile
